# Cultural Landscapes Inventory: Clement Redding Farm, Eisenhower National Historic Site (2025 edition; the 2004 edition is read in other definition files). The physical history is on PDF pp. 41-46 (printed pp. 36-41).
# The farm in 1863 belonged to the Sandoes (David Sandoe bought it in 1861); features built or changed after 1863 (the bank barn, the brick house, sheds) are use none, the Warren survey of 1868-69 is read as a map of the years after the battle (use indirect).
# Workstream bt-web, task nps-landscape, third milestone.

@ NPS-EISE-CLI-REDDING-2025
42 | buildings | Clement Redding farm before 1799: Thomas Douglas had added three log structures, a two-story house, a kitchen and a barn (Federal Direct Tax of 1798) | - | pre-1863 (log structures standing before 1799; the inventory gives no later date of removal for the kitchen and barn) | stated | The inventory says that before his death in 1799 Thomas Douglas had added three log structures, a two-story house, a kitchen and a barn, citing the Federal Direct Tax of 1798 | Before Thomas's death in 1799, he had added three log structures including, a two-story house, a kitchen, and a barn (Federal Direct Tax, 1798).
45 | fields | The Clement Redding farm in 1863: hit by artillery fire and stripped of farm resources by trampling and looting, while Farms 1, 2 and 3 escaped the most intense fighting | - | 1863-07 | stated | The inventory says that while Farms 1, 2 and 3 of the Eisenhower site escaped the most intense fighting of the battle in 1863, the Clement Redding Farm was subjected to artillery fire and losses of farm resources through trampling and looting by the soldiers | While Farms #1, 2, and 3 of the Eisenhower NHS escaped the most intense fighting of the Battle of Gettysburg in 1863, the Clement Redding Farm was subjected to artillery fire and losses of farm resources due to trampling and looting by the soldiers.
45 | earthworks | Breastworks across the east and south edges of Farms 1 and 2; a stone wall on the east border of the Redding farm that may date to the Civil War era, with no conclusive evidence that it was used for cover | - | 1863-07 (hedged: may date to the Civil War era; no conclusive evidence of use in the battle) | hedged | The inventory says breastworks extended across the east and south perimeters of Farms 1 and 2 and that a stone wall along the eastern border of the Clement Redding Farm may date to the Civil War era although there is no conclusive evidence that it was used for cover during the battle | Breastworks extended across the east and south perimeters of Farms #1 and #2. A stone wall along the eastern border of the Clement Redding Farm may date to the Civil War era, although there is no conclusive evidence that it was used for cover during battle.
45 | roads | The Warren survey party's maps, soon after the war, show the pre-nineteenth-century circulation system of all four Eisenhower farms | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren survey; the circulation system is older than the nineteenth century) | stated | The inventory says the maps completed by the Warren survey party soon after the Civil War ended show evidence of the pre-nineteenth-century circulation system for all four farms of the Eisenhower site that remain evident today | Maps completed by the Warren survey party soon after the Civil War ended, show evidence of the pre-nineteenth-century circulation system for all four farms of the Eisenhower NHS that remain evident today.
45 | damage | After the battle all farm occupants of the Eisenhower site except the Sandoes of the Redding farm filed claims, which recorded loss of livestock, acres of crops, clothing, foodstuff, equipment, fencing and damage to farm land and gardens | - | 1863-07 (the losses the claims recorded; the claims were filed after the battle) | stated | The inventory says that occupants of all the farms of the Eisenhower site filed reimbursement claims except the Sandoes of the Clement Redding Farm and that the claims recorded loss of livestock, acres of crops, clothing, foodstuff, equipment, fencing and damage to farm land and gardens | Occupants of all the farms included today in the Eisenhower NHS filed reimbursement claims except the Sandoes of Clement Redding Farm. Claims recorded loss of livestock, acres of crops, clothing, foodstuff, equipment, fencing and damage to farm land and gardens.
45 | buildings | The large bank barn of the Redding farm was built by Sandoe between the mid-1860s and 1885 | - | !mid-1860s to 1885 (the bank barn, built after the battle) | stated | The inventory says that between the mid-1860s and 1885 Sandoe built the large bank barn | Between the mid-1860s and 1885, Sandoe built the large bank barn.
45 | buildings | The Warren map shows four other wooden structures on the Redding farm and an orchard west of the farm buildings | - | ~1868-1869 (Warren map; the inventory does not say which of the four structures stood in 1863) | stated | The inventory says the Warren Map illustrates four other wooden structures on the property as well as an orchard west of the farm buildings | The Warren Map illustrates four other wooden structures on the property as well as an orchard west of the farm buildings.
46 | buildings | A log structure down slope of the 1901 house site, sometimes called the original dwelling but most likely a kitchen built by Thomas Douglass in the late eighteenth century, demolished by Redding | - | pre-1863 (hedged: most likely a kitchen of the late eighteenth century; the demolition came after 1901) | hedged | The inventory says the house site of 1898 to 1901 was down slope from a log structure sometimes purported to be the original dwelling but most likely a kitchen built by Thomas Douglass in the late eighteenth century, which Redding demolished once his new house was complete | The site was down slope from a log structure sometimes purported to be the original dwelling but most likely a kitchen built by Thomas Douglass during the late-eighteenth century. Once the large, red brick, Queen Anne style farmhouse was complete, Redding demolished this older structure.
46 | buildings | Between 1898 and 1901 Redding graded land below the ridgeline for a new brick house with a small stone retaining wall | - | !1898-1901 (grading and the new brick farmhouse; after the battle) | stated | The inventory says that between 1898 and 1901 Redding substantially graded land just below a ridgeline to create a flat area for a new house, using a small stone retaining wall | Between 1898 and 1901, Redding substantially graded land just below a ridgeline to create a flat area upon which to build a new house employing a small, stone retaining wall.
